NAME
    pkg - manage packages in the m package subsystem

SYNOPSIS
    pkg install <package-spec>...
    pkg uninstall <package-spec>...
    pkg versions <package-class>
    pkg depend <package-spec>...
    pkg default [-u] [--] <package-spec>
    pkg provider default <facility> [<provider-selector>]
    pkg provider default -u [--] <facility>
    pkg provider bind <consumer> <facility> [<provider-selector>]
    pkg provider bind -u [--] <consumer> <facility>
    pkg requirement list <package-spec>
    pkg requirement resolve <facility> <constraint>...

DESCRIPTION
    pkg is the public package command of the technical m layer. Package catalog
    data is obtained from the current pkg-catalog source; installed package content
    is managed beneath $m_PKG_DIR.

    Package operations use the real package pipeline for catalog/repository
    resolution, artifact integrity, download, extraction, integration, defaults,
    and launch/state integration as applicable.

PACKAGE OPERANDS
    Package operands use the current forms:

        <package>
        <package>@<version>
        <package>!<osarch>
        <package>@<version>!<osarch>

    A version or osarch suffix is optional where the selected subcommand permits
    it. When installation omits a version, pkg resolves the applicable version from
    the current catalog for the requested package/platform class.

    Provider selectors use the same four package-spec shapes. An omitted version
    expresses a provider-package selection that follows that package's current
    default when resolved; an explicit version expresses a pinned provider version.

COMMANDS
    install <package-spec>...
        Validate the complete request list before dependency planning. If any
        package specification is syntactically invalid, fail before catalog
        resolution or package installation. Otherwise discover recursive
        dependencies with pkg depend, prepend the returned exact dependency
        concretes to the original request list, and install the resulting sequence
        dependency-first.

        The original requested package specifications remain requests and are
        resolved by the installer when reached. Dependency operands are already
        concrete. An already installed concrete is not reinstalled.

    uninstall <package-spec>...
        Remove one or more installed package versions. When a version is omitted,
        the current/default version is selected when available; a single installed
        version may also be resolved directly.

    versions <package-class>
        List installed concrete package identities for one package/platform class.
        A version suffix is not accepted for this query.

    depend <package-spec>...
        Discover and resolve only the recursive facility dependencies of the
        requested packages without installing anything. Requested root packages are
        not printed unless the same concrete is also selected as a dependency of
        another request.

        Dependency output is one exact concrete identity per line in
        dependency-first order. Consumer binding has precedence over the system
        facility default. Without either selector, an already planned compatible
        provider is preferred, followed by an unambiguous compatible installed
        provider. Only when no compatible planned or installed provider exists may
        the planner add a catalog provider; multiple compatible provider packages
        are ambiguous and fail rather than being ranked silently. Such an
        ambiguity reports reason=provider-ambiguous together with the affected
        facility, combined constraints, target osarch and exact compatible provider
        candidates so the operator can configure an explicit provider selection.

        The command is read-only with respect to package/provider state. Catalog
        cache refresh and invocation-private temporary snapshot materialization are
        allowed.

    default <package-spec>
        With an explicit version, select that installed version as the current
        default for the package/platform class. Without a version, print the
        current concrete default identity.

    default -u [--] <package-class>
        Clear the current default binding for the package/platform class. A version
        suffix is not accepted when -u is used.

    provider default <facility>
        Print the configured system default provider selector for the facility.

    provider default <facility> <provider-selector>
        Set the system default provider selector for the facility. Commands declared
        by that facility are reconciled into bin/ext or bin/ext-<osarch> according
        to provider-selector and package-default semantics. Every later m bootstrap
        also re-resolves the facility default and applies the selected provider's
        facility-env projection when that provider is resolvable for the active
        platform class. Already-running processes are unchanged.

    provider default -u [--] <facility>
        Remove the configured system default provider selector and its owned global
        facility command projection.

    provider bind <consumer> <facility>
        Print the configured provider selector bound to the consumer/facility pair.

    provider bind <consumer> <facility> <provider-selector>
        Set the provider selector bound to the consumer/facility pair.

    provider bind -u [--] <consumer> <facility>
        Remove the consumer binding. A package consumer then uses the system facility
        default when configured; if neither binding nor default exists, runtime
        resolution may use an unambiguous compatible installed provider.

    requirement list <package-spec>
        Resolve the selected package/version/platform definition from the current
        catalog and print its declared dependency lines without downloading or
        extracting the package artifact. An empty successful result means that the
        selected package definition declares no facility dependencies.

    requirement resolve <facility> <constraint>...
        Resolve the configured system facility default using the normal global
        package-class/osarch semantics and verify that the selected installed
        concrete declares <facility> at a compatibility satisfying every supplied
        constraint.

        On success, print the selected concrete provider identity. The query is
        read-only: it does not install providers, choose among installed providers
        implicitly, change a facility default or create a consumer binding. A
        status-1 failure identifies whether the facility default is unconfigured,
        unresolvable or resolves to an incompatible provider.

        Compatibility constraints use the package dependency language, for example:

            =25
            >=21
            >=21 <26

NATIVE-COMMAND MIGRATION
    Installed package concretes contain copies of launcher and package env
    definitions from the catalog revision used during installation. After
    migrating m from #!/usr/bin/env m to native command entrypoints, older
    installed concretes remain incompatible until they are reinstalled.

    Coordinate a maintenance window for the affected packages and services;
    do not publish the new m dispatcher independently of the compatible
    catalog. Before any removal, inventory every exact installed concrete
    with pkg versions <package-class>, record defaults with pkg default
    <package-class>, and preserve provider bindings and system service
    registrations. Verify that each exact version can be restored from the
    selected new catalog, including artifact availability/integrity, and
    prepare restorable backups of both the installation and package state
    (user, system and any service state instances). Inspect unmanaged mutable
    data inside the package root as well.

    Stop affected consumers/services. For each recorded exact concrete use:

        m pkg uninstall <package>@<version>[!<osarch>]
        m pkg install   <package>@<version>[!<osarch>]

    After all required versions of a class are installed, restore only a
    previously recorded current/default selection:

        m pkg default <package>@<selected-version>[!<osarch>]

    Leave previously unselected classes without a default. Confirm native
    cmd/env content, command execution, managed configuration and service
    state before restarting consumers. Respect provider references: the
    manager can refuse removal of a still-referenced provider. Handle
    consumer/provider dependency order accordingly and do not force-delete
    directories or edit immutable installed concretions.

    Each uninstall/install is a separate operation; the complete migration
    is not transactional. If artifact acquisition, provider compatibility or
    reinstall fails, stop and reconcile explicitly using the recorded state
    and verified compatible backup. Do not replace an unavailable exact
    version with latest and do not assume pkg depend proves artifact access.

STATE
    Mutable package state is resolved through state-path rather than reconstructed
    by callers. Package HOME and configuration used at launch are user-scoped state.

    m-managed package configuration normally uses the reserved .m subnamespace
    inside the package conf area. Provider bindings are the explicit exception:
    a system-scoped consumer binding is stored as binding/<facility> in that
    package's system conf area and contains exactly one provider selector.

FILES
    $m_PKG_DIR
        Managed package store root. pkg install does not create this directory for
        an invocation whose request list contains a syntactically invalid operand.

ENVIRONMENT
    Requires a nonempty m_ROOT inherited from an initialized m environment.
    From an uninitialized host shell, invoke m pkg [arguments].


EXIT STATUS
    0   The requested operation succeeded.
    1   The requested package operation could not be completed after a valid
        invocation, or the requested installed class/default could not be resolved
        where required.
    2   The command or subcommand invocation is invalid. For pkg install, this
        includes any syntactically invalid package specification in the original
        request list; no dependency planning or installation is attempted.

    Individual package execution launched after integration has its own exit status;
    that is not the exit status of this pkg management command.

EXAMPLES
    Install the catalog-selected version of a package:

        pkg install nodejs

    List installed versions for the current platform class:

        pkg versions nodejs

    Query the current default concrete identity:

        pkg default nodejs

    Clear the default binding:

        pkg default -u nodejs

    Select Temurin as the system provider for the java facility:

        pkg provider default java temurin

    Pin Maven to a specific Temurin version:

        pkg provider bind maven java temurin@25.0.4.1+1

    Inspect the requirements declared by the catalog-selected Keycloak package:

        pkg requirement list keycloak

    Verify that the current system java provider satisfies a global/build requirement:

        pkg requirement resolve java '>=21' '<26'

